  <iati-identifier>NL-KVK-27378529-PSI14UG08</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="NL-KVK-27378529" secondary-reporter="0" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Netherlands Enterprise Agency</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The first integrated pig production project of Uganda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Pork meat is widely consumed in Uganda however, the sector is not organised and underdeveloped. Modern husbandry techniques are lacking and the small rural farmers are keeping pigs with genetics originating from breeds imported almost twenty years ago. This project will address two opportunities which have been reported for the pig sector in Uganda:1.	Pig breeding and pig multiplication centres the growing foreign community in Uganda has increased the demand for pork and bacon and2.	Abattoirs for pigs. The production and sales of (premium quality) pork meat targeting the foreign and upper class of Uganda is the projects main activity.The partners are convinced that the first critical steps of progress in a country like Uganda would be improving production techniques with improved (and not top-end) genetic material and building a know-how base.Applicant, SUIN, from Belgium is involved in pig production since 1995. The companys founder has developed a special pig breed called Duroc d&apos;olives and runs a closed pig farm with 1,200 sows producing fattening pigs.Local partner, Greenfields, from Uganda has a successful track record of over ten years in the fishery sector through a previous PSOM Tilapia project.Third partner Marc Thyssen is a Belgian agricultural engineer experienced in the animal feed business and in meat processing.Together, the three partners possess all knowledge and experience needed to operate the new farm which will be Ugandas first AI centre, pig abattoir, breeding farm and special pig feed formulation laboratory. Animal Welfare conditions, implemented along EU standards, will get full attention through cooperation with a local Pig Farmers association and will be monitored by the International Livestock Research Institute.</narrative>
  </description>
